WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.000 --> 00:00:04.580
Welcome back to Curious Archive.

00:00:04.580 --> 00:00:10.960
This video is the finale of my two-part series
on the biology of Darwin IV — an alien planet

00:00:10.960 --> 00:00:16.840
explored in the book ‘Expedition’ by Wayne
Barlowe, which I have a link to in the description.

00:00:16.840 --> 00:00:23.380
On this perilous world, deadly predators,
strange herbivores, and uncategorizable oddities

00:00:23.380 --> 00:00:25.800
are still waiting to be discovered.

00:00:25.800 --> 00:00:31.740
So, follow along on this firsthand account
of an expedition across a spectacular planet

00:00:31.740 --> 00:00:37.700
— a journey that will end with an encounter
with a mysterious observer…

00:00:37.700 --> 00:00:41.360
I am now one week into my survey of Darwin
IV.

00:00:41.360 --> 00:00:47.090
To the east of the Amoebic Sea, I encounter
the monolithic skull of an Emperor Sea Strider

00:00:47.090 --> 00:00:49.289
that appears to have died long ago.

00:00:49.289 --> 00:00:54.780
I notice a herd of short-legged herbivores
called Littoralopes taking cover at the skull’s

00:00:54.780 --> 00:00:55.780
base.

00:00:55.780 --> 00:00:57.680
I soon realize what they’re hiding from.

00:00:57.680 --> 00:01:00.410
A shadow swoops overhead.

00:01:00.410 --> 00:01:02.930
The Littoralopes scatter in all directions.

00:01:02.930 --> 00:01:08.110
In the resulting panic, a lone Littoralope
is knocked to the ground with a heavy thud.

00:01:08.110 --> 00:01:11.140
And the shadow swoops in from above.

00:01:11.140 --> 00:01:16.790
This aerial predator is called a Skewer, and
it is the uncontested ruler of the skies of

00:01:16.790 --> 00:01:22.110
Darwin IV — with a wingspan of almost 50
feet, or 15 meters.

00:01:22.110 --> 00:01:27.590
At a glance, the monstrous Skewers almost
resemble biological stealth aircrafts.

00:01:27.590 --> 00:01:32.320
While the idea of an animal converging on
a jet-like shape might seem surprising, the

00:01:32.320 --> 00:01:36.760
real-life anatomy of certain birds of prey
and stealth aircrafts are quite similar due

00:01:36.760 --> 00:01:39.549
to the universal constraints of aerodynamics.

00:01:39.549 --> 00:01:44.420
The Skewers take this convergence to a new
level, however, through their ability to internally

00:01:44.420 --> 00:01:49.259
create methane gas which then is combusted
within the four jet-pods located on their

00:01:49.259 --> 00:01:50.350
wings for propulsion.

00:01:50.350 --> 00:01:54.700
And when a Skewer chooses a target, the hunt
is already over.

00:01:54.700 --> 00:02:00.150
I watch as a Skewer rises up into the air,
and pitches downward with its stinger pointed

00:02:00.150 --> 00:02:01.150
forwards.

00:02:01.150 --> 00:02:03.500
In an instant, it impales its prey.

00:02:03.500 --> 00:02:06.840
I count myself lucky I’m too small to be
its target.

00:02:06.840 --> 00:02:12.280
Yet as I watch the Skewer fly off, I get the
strange feeling I’m not alone.

00:02:12.280 --> 00:02:16.180
Even as I continue on, I feel like something
is watching me…

00:02:16.180 --> 00:02:21.459
Putting these feelings aside for now, further
east, I reach a sprawling flatland where the

00:02:21.459 --> 00:02:24.450
margins of the Amoebic Sea have receded.

00:02:24.450 --> 00:02:30.300
Within this region, which was once the sea
floor, I spot the Sac-Back: a bulky, red lifeform

00:02:30.300 --> 00:02:31.870
with peculiar anatomy.

00:02:31.870 --> 00:02:37.400
The species get their name from a sac used
to store predigested food from the Amoebic

00:02:37.400 --> 00:02:41.290
Sea, almost like the world’s weirdest camel’s
hump.

00:02:41.290 --> 00:02:46.450
Even stranger, however, is that this Sac-Back’s
stored food isn’t for itself, but for its

00:02:46.450 --> 00:02:47.450
mate.

00:02:47.450 --> 00:02:52.769
Under the sand, a second Sac-Back has buried
itself up to its head along with its eggs,

00:02:52.769 --> 00:02:56.360
and relies on its above-ground partner to
bring it food.

00:02:56.360 --> 00:03:01.330
Strange as this might sound, Earth Hornbills
exhibit a highly-convergent behavior.

00:03:01.330 --> 00:03:06.740
When a Hornbill needs to incubate its eggs,
it completely seals itself within its nests,

00:03:06.740 --> 00:03:12.379
while its mate dutifully brings them regurgitated
food for a period lasting up to four months.

00:03:12.379 --> 00:03:17.930
It’s a risky endeavor which requires a tremendous
amount of trust between the two parents.

00:03:17.930 --> 00:03:22.850
So, it is strangely moving that such a social
behavior would arise not just on Earth, but

00:03:22.850 --> 00:03:25.930
on a planet as perilous as Darwin IV.

00:03:25.930 --> 00:03:32.950
To the south, I find myself face-to-face with
a rather unsightly creature; this is a Bladderhorn,

00:03:32.950 --> 00:03:37.350
an animal that is so bizarre it is at first
difficult to categorize.

00:03:37.350 --> 00:03:42.299
It lives at the base of an equatorial mountain
range, and thankfully seems to feed on plants

00:03:42.299 --> 00:03:44.269
rather than other lifeforms.

00:03:44.269 --> 00:03:50.470
With their mid-sized grazing niche and bellowing
calls, these creatures are the elk of Darwin

00:03:50.470 --> 00:03:51.470
IV.

00:03:51.470 --> 00:03:53.900
Another Bladderhorn approaches from behind.

00:03:53.900 --> 00:03:58.920
The first one isn’t happy with this invader,
and bellows while it inflates its glowing

00:03:58.920 --> 00:04:00.240
horns.

00:04:00.240 --> 00:04:03.040
When this fails, the two butt heads.

00:04:03.040 --> 00:04:07.959
Like birds of paradise, these Bladderhorns
appear to engage in bizarre displays over

00:04:07.959 --> 00:04:10.010
territory and mating rights.

00:04:10.010 --> 00:04:15.120
Indeed, many birds of paradise rituals are
just as strange as the alien Bladderhorns

00:04:15.120 --> 00:04:16.940
— which is saying something.

00:04:16.940 --> 00:04:21.650
I decide to leave the Bladderhorns to it,
and see what other lifeforms dwell in this

00:04:21.650 --> 00:04:23.090
mountain range…

00:04:23.090 --> 00:04:28.710
Scaling the foothills of the equatorial mountains,
I find myself caught in heavy rainfall.

00:04:28.710 --> 00:04:33.419
Taking shelter under an overhanging rock,
I realize two leathery brown creatures are

00:04:33.419 --> 00:04:37.070
huddled nearby, also hiding from the storm.

00:04:37.070 --> 00:04:42.290
These are Keeled Sliders, peaceful mountainous
herbivores which thankfully aren’t aggravated

00:04:42.290 --> 00:04:43.810
by my presence.

00:04:43.810 --> 00:04:48.860
Like mountain goats, Keeled Sliders can maneuver
down steep slopes with remarkable ease using

00:04:48.860 --> 00:04:51.010
their specialized front limbs.

00:04:51.010 --> 00:04:54.280
Yet these Keeled Sliders are keeping remarkably
still.

00:04:54.280 --> 00:04:58.890
A scan detects 20 small life signs in the
small cave behind the pair.

00:04:58.890 --> 00:05:02.430
It seems these are parents guarding a nest
of newborns.

00:05:02.430 --> 00:05:07.080
I don’t want to disrupt them, and since
the rain is letting up, I’ll be on my way.

00:05:07.080 --> 00:05:11.620
After a grueling climb, I reach the summit
of the equatorial mountains.

00:05:11.620 --> 00:05:16.840
Looking to the horizon as I catch my breath,
I spot a herd of four-legged grazers on the

00:05:16.840 --> 00:05:18.640
peaks beside me.

00:05:18.640 --> 00:05:23.500
These are Springwings, and while they look
ordinary from a distance, Springwings are

00:05:23.500 --> 00:05:26.530
the champions of the chilly alpine skies.

00:05:26.530 --> 00:05:29.310
A gust of wind howls overhead.

00:05:29.310 --> 00:05:35.750
I look up and see an adult Springwing leaping
into the air above me, unfurling gliding appendages

00:05:35.750 --> 00:05:39.150
that allow it soar out into the abyss.

00:05:39.150 --> 00:05:44.410
Watching a cow-sized organism take flight
is a majestic and bizarre sight — made possible

00:05:44.410 --> 00:05:48.270
thanks to Darwin IV’s thick atmosphere and
low gravity.

00:05:48.270 --> 00:05:53.410
Their fixed-wing structure resembles the body
plan of Draco lizards, which can glide in

00:05:53.410 --> 00:05:54.680
a similar manner…

00:05:54.680 --> 00:05:56.360
although they’re much smaller.

00:05:56.360 --> 00:06:00.449
Just as I’m about to depart, I notice something
moving on the rock above me.

00:06:00.449 --> 00:06:02.460
But it quickly recedes from view.

00:06:02.460 --> 00:06:05.330
It seems I am still being observed…

00:06:05.330 --> 00:06:10.250
Heading northwards, I’m drawn to a commotion
caused by a stampeding herd.

00:06:10.250 --> 00:06:14.910
These behemoths are called Unths — creatures
named for the sound they make when they exhale

00:06:14.910 --> 00:06:16.810
through their dorsal vents.

00:06:16.810 --> 00:06:22.320
Heavy-set herbivores, Unths are the lumbering
bison of Darwin IV, who rely their herd to

00:06:22.320 --> 00:06:24.910
stay safe while they browse for plants.

00:06:24.910 --> 00:06:27.289
Yet Unth herds aren’t without conflict.

00:06:27.289 --> 00:06:32.410
Before my eyes, two Unths engage in an earth-shaking
battle for dominance.

00:06:32.410 --> 00:06:36.920
Throwing their full weight into each other,
their posturing reminds me of Musk Ox clashes

00:06:36.920 --> 00:06:38.210
on Earth.

00:06:38.210 --> 00:06:43.380
Yet despite the occasional clash, the Unth
herd seems remarkably tight knit, as they

00:06:43.380 --> 00:06:47.330
are united against a common enemy — the
weather.

00:06:47.330 --> 00:06:51.509
This far to the north, nothing is more dangerous
than the dropping temperatures.

00:06:51.509 --> 00:06:56.440
I’ve now reached a windswept region where
the ground is covered in near-permanent ice

00:06:56.440 --> 00:07:00.250
— and most lifeforms on Darwin IV can’t
survive for long.

00:07:00.250 --> 00:07:06.639
Yet even in this extreme biome, some creatures
have found a way to adapt to the cold.

00:07:06.639 --> 00:07:11.610
Close by, I notice an organism which at first
glance appears to be in distress, as it is

00:07:11.610 --> 00:07:14.830
half buried in the snow at an awkward angle.

00:07:14.830 --> 00:07:20.150
Looking closer, however, I realize that this
creature — called a Tundra-Plow, is actually

00:07:20.150 --> 00:07:22.410
scouring under the surface for food.

00:07:22.410 --> 00:07:27.690
Plenty of Earth animals forage for food under
the ground, and many others tunnel under the

00:07:27.690 --> 00:07:29.930
snow for similar reasons.

00:07:29.930 --> 00:07:35.300
But the Tundra-Plow makes such behaviors look
extremely tame by comparison: staying halfway

00:07:35.300 --> 00:07:39.430
underground in a manner that looks like a
faceplant for long periods.

00:07:39.430 --> 00:07:45.500
It’s a somewhat absurd method of survival,
but it appears to be working reasonably well.

00:07:45.500 --> 00:07:50.100
Trekking as far north as I can safely travel,
I’ve reached a glacier cap where only the

00:07:50.100 --> 00:07:52.520
most extreme species can live.

00:07:52.520 --> 00:07:58.580
Here, I find the shivering Icecrawlers — small,
nondescript organisms that slowly make their

00:07:58.580 --> 00:08:03.990
way across the glacier’s surface, feeding
on microorganisms with a mouth on the underside

00:08:03.990 --> 00:08:05.110
of the shell.

00:08:05.110 --> 00:08:10.370
With their segmented bodies, the Icecrawlers
have converged on a body plan similar to giant

00:08:10.370 --> 00:08:14.629
isopods — crustaceans that can also endure
frigid temperatures.

00:08:14.629 --> 00:08:19.509
I’m about to turn back when perhaps the
strangest lifeform I’ve seen yet dashes

00:08:19.509 --> 00:08:21.590
across my field of vision.

00:08:21.590 --> 00:08:27.480
This is a Rimerunner — a fast-moving creature
that hops over the ice on its single foot.

00:08:27.480 --> 00:08:32.719
While a single-legged animal is highly unusual,
it’s not altogether unheard of in the natural

00:08:32.719 --> 00:08:38.490
world — as most bivalve mollusks like clams
also only possess a single foot, which they

00:08:38.490 --> 00:08:40.210
use as a bizarre form of motion.

00:08:40.210 --> 00:08:44.339
It’s likely the Rimerunner evolved from
a similar organism.

00:08:44.339 --> 00:08:48.600
Even stranger than this creature’s foot,
however, is the parachute-shaped structure

00:08:48.600 --> 00:08:50.330
on the front of its head.

00:08:50.330 --> 00:08:54.700
At first, I thought this orange structure
was a separate lifeform, but it’s actually

00:08:54.700 --> 00:08:58.470
connected to the Rimerunner via a thin neural
cable.

00:08:58.470 --> 00:09:02.860
Looking closely at the floating organ, it
seems it’s a ‘sensory packet’ that helps

00:09:02.860 --> 00:09:08.260
the Rimerunner better navigate through echolocation
— almost like an organic radar dish.

00:09:08.260 --> 00:09:10.890
A unique lifeform indeed.

00:09:10.890 --> 00:09:15.320
Returning to the warmth of the southern reaches,
I worry I won’t find creatures as strange

00:09:15.320 --> 00:09:16.589
as the Rimerunner.

00:09:16.589 --> 00:09:21.519
My worries quickly fade, however, when I spot
two crescent-shaped lifeforms hovering in

00:09:21.519 --> 00:09:22.750
the distance.

00:09:22.750 --> 00:09:25.940
These wrinkled oddities are Rugose Floaters.

00:09:25.940 --> 00:09:29.610
Rather lazy creatures, they fly in wide, slow
circles.

00:09:29.610 --> 00:09:35.830
A scan tells me they’re able to stay aloft
thanks to internal sacs of buoyant gas.

00:09:35.830 --> 00:09:41.339
This leads me to wonder: if the bulky Rugose
Floaters can fly using this method, might

00:09:41.339 --> 00:09:44.330
a true giant be hidden in the skies?

00:09:44.330 --> 00:09:46.019
I soon get my answer.

00:09:46.019 --> 00:09:50.720
The largest airborne creature of Darwin IV
breaches through the clouds.

00:09:50.720 --> 00:09:56.720
This is an Ebony Blisterwing — an animal
that is longer than even the Emperor Sea Striders

00:09:56.720 --> 00:10:01.260
I glimpsed earlier in the expedition — although
much lighter and more fragile.

00:10:01.260 --> 00:10:06.880
While I’m only able to glimpse this creature
for a moment, I can theorize the Ebony Blisterwing

00:10:06.880 --> 00:10:12.200
stays aloft using the bubble-like bladders
on its wings and body, which keep it afloat

00:10:12.200 --> 00:10:13.800
like giant balloons.

00:10:13.800 --> 00:10:19.190
And while the diet of this majestic being
is unknown, I suspect it feeds on airborne

00:10:19.190 --> 00:10:24.890
microscopic organisms — filtering them from
the skies much like a baleen whale filters

00:10:24.890 --> 00:10:27.280
zooplankton.

00:10:27.280 --> 00:10:30.410
It is now my final day on Darwin IV.

00:10:30.410 --> 00:10:34.740
Just as I’m returning to my launch site,
from the skies descends the most important

00:10:34.740 --> 00:10:36.630
lifeform I’ve seen.

00:10:36.630 --> 00:10:43.010
This is an Eosapien, a highly unusual yet
clearly complex floating organism that hovers

00:10:43.010 --> 00:10:47.260
in the air, and seems to be observing me with
quiet intelligence.

00:10:47.260 --> 00:10:52.890
I realize this is my mysterious follower — and
it is Darwin IV’s version of intelligent

00:10:52.890 --> 00:10:53.890
life.

00:10:53.890 --> 00:10:56.779
In its hands, it holds a carved, defensive
weapon.

00:10:56.779 --> 00:11:01.660
Not wanting to alarm the Eopsaien with any
sudden movements, I sit for a while and map

00:11:01.660 --> 00:11:03.089
out its anatomy.

00:11:03.089 --> 00:11:07.830
The Eosapien’s brain appears to be housed
somewhere in its central body, likely close

00:11:07.830 --> 00:11:09.980
to its sensory organs.

00:11:09.980 --> 00:11:15.670
Furthermore, the Eosapien also possesses flexible
digits that enable tool-crafting.

00:11:15.670 --> 00:11:21.019
While right now it seems Eosapies are still
crafting rudimentary tools, perhaps long into

00:11:21.019 --> 00:11:24.661
the future, they will build a civilization
here on Darwin IV.

00:11:24.661 --> 00:11:30.980
For a species like the Eospaien, the future
is full of exciting possibilities.

00:11:30.980 --> 00:11:34.220
Seemingly content with its own observations,
the Eospaiens departs.

00:11:34.220 --> 00:11:41.160
Watching the Eospaien leave reminds me that
the time has come for me also to return home.

00:11:41.160 --> 00:11:46.390
But if you enjoyed this expedition across
Darwin IV as much as I did, I once again encourage

00:11:46.390 --> 00:11:50.660
you to purchase the book and check out more
of Wayne Barlowe’s incredible work.

00:11:50.660 --> 00:11:53.220
As always, links are in the description.

00:11:53.220 --> 00:11:55.190
Also, thanks for watching.

00:11:55.190 --> 00:11:59.920
If you enjoyed this entry, please lend your
support to help make these videos possible

00:11:59.920 --> 00:12:05.950
by liking, subscribing, and hitting the notification
icon to stay up to date on all things Curious.

00:12:05.950 --> 00:12:12.259
See you in the next video.

